WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.497
Chapter three. Wampum: when the tools changed.

00:00:05.847 --> 00:00:10.314
On America's northeast coast, people made beads from shells:

00:00:10.314 --> 00:00:14.633
white from the whelk, purple from the quahog clam. Wampum.

00:00:14.983 --> 00:00:21.230
Each bead was cut, drilled and polished by hand, with stone. Slow work.

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:26.333
Strung into belts, wampum sealed treaties between nations.

00:00:26.684 --> 00:00:30.121
European colonists arrived with almost no coins.

00:00:30.121 --> 00:00:34.919
Inland nations took wampum for furs, so colonists used it as money.

00:00:34.919 --> 00:00:39.646
In sixteen thirty-seven, Massachusetts made it legal small change:

00:00:39.646 --> 00:00:41.150
six beads to a penny.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:47.186
The newcomers also brought iron. Coastal makers switched to iron drill points:

00:00:47.186 --> 00:00:52.726
far faster than stone. Beads multiplied. Rough ones and fakes poured in too.

00:00:53.076 --> 00:00:56.418
In Dutch New Amsterdam, a beaver pelt cost about

00:00:56.418 --> 00:01:01.360
six hundred and forty beads in sixteen forty-one. By sixteen sixty-two:

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:06.790
three thousand two hundred. Same pelt. Five times the beads. That's inflation:

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:09.853
the pelt didn't change. The beads got cheap.

00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:14.145
If you saved wampum, or earned it, it bought less and less.

00:01:14.145 --> 00:01:19.223
You did nothing wrong. In sixteen fifty-eight, the town's leaders complained

00:01:19.223 --> 00:01:22.697
that bakers and brewers would hardly take the beads.

00:01:23.048 --> 00:01:27.288
In sixteen sixty-one, with Boston now minting silver coins,

00:01:27.288 --> 00:01:32.823
Massachusetts dropped wampum as legal money. Wampum belts still matter today.

00:01:32.823 --> 00:01:34.836
But as money, it faded away.

00:01:35.186 --> 00:01:41.941
In Ulm Hollow, Wren found a cave. Here, the cave was an iron drill. Same story.

00:01:41.941 --> 00:01:46.472
Next: cowries, the money that got too heavy to carry.

